### Expected results

The framework's `SemanticsRole` reaches the platform's accessibility API:
- **Windows (UIA / MSAA):** MenuBar / Menu / MenuItem (`ROLE_SYSTEM_MENUBAR` / `MENUPOPUP` / `MENUITEM`).
- **macOS:** `NSAccessibilityMenuBarRole` / `MenuRole` / `MenuItemRole`.
- **Linux (ATK):** `ATK_ROLE_MENU_BAR` / `MENU` / `MENU_ITEM`.

The screen reader then speaks, for example, "File, menu item" and "Rename, menu item, 1 of 2".

### Actual results

Every node gets the role the desktop bridge derives from its flags alone: a button, a group or static text. The roles menuBar, menu, menuItem, menuItemCheckbox and menuItemRadio never reach the platform. Neither do tab, tabBar, tabPanel, dialog, alertDialog, table, row, cell, columnHeader, comboBox or spinButton. So a menu bar and its items are announced as plain buttons, and a popup menu as a list of buttons.

(We found this by reading the 3.44.9 sources below while auditing our desktop app's menus for Narrator; we haven't attached a verbatim screen-reader transcript.)

### Cause

1. **The embedder API carries no role.** `shell/platform/embedder/embedder.h` has no role field in `FlutterSemanticsNode2`; the file doesn't mention a role anywhere. So the `SemanticsRole` set in the framework (`lib/ui/semantics.dart`, `enum SemanticsRole`) is dropped before it reaches the Windows, macOS and Linux embedders.
2. **The shared desktop bridge picks a role from flags only.** `shell/platform/common/accessibility_bridge.cc`, `AccessibilityBridge::SetRoleFromFlutterUpdate`, maps:
   - `is_button` → `kButton`; `is_text_field` → `kTextField`; `is_header` → `kHeader`;
   - `is_image` → `kImage`; `is_link` → `kLink`;
   - checked/toggled/slider → `kRadioButton` / `kCheckBox` / `kSwitch` / `kSlider`;
   - otherwise `kStaticText` or `kGroup`.

   No branch can produce `kMenuBar`, `kMenu`, `kMenuItem`, `kTab`, `kDialog` and so on.

The framework already assigns some of these roles: `PopupMenuItem` (`menuItem`, `menuItemCheckbox`), and `DropdownButton`'s menu and items. #165682 tracks wiring `MenuAnchor` / `MenuBar` / `MenuItemButton`. On desktop, that wiring has no effect until the embedder API and the bridge carry the role.

### Proposed fix

1. Add a `FlutterSemanticsRole role` field to `FlutterSemanticsNode2`, filled from the semantics node's role.
2. In `SetRoleFromFlutterUpdate`, map the role first and fall back to the flags when it is `none`:
   - menuBar → kMenuBar, menu → kMenu, menuItem → kMenuItem;
   - menuItemCheckbox → kMenuItemCheckBox, menuItemRadio → kMenuItemRadio;
   - tab → kTab, tabBar → kTabList, tabPanel → kTabPanel;
   - dialog → kDialog, alertDialog → kAlertDialog;
   - table → kTable, row → kRow, cell → kCell, columnHeader → kColumnHeader;
   - spinButton → kSpinButton, comboBox → kComboBoxGrouping.

   AXPlatformNodeWin, AXPlatformNodeMac and the ATK layer already know these roles.

Related: #165682 (framework wiring for `MenuAnchor`), #101289 (Windows dialogs), #192782 (`Semantics.hint` not exposed on desktop).

### Flutter version

3.44.9 (stable), on Windows 11 and macOS 27.

### Steps to reproduce

This needs two platform views: one full-screen behind everything, and a second one that is still laid out but entirely clipped away by its parent.

1. Build a `Stack` on iOS with, in this order:
   - a full-screen platform view (a native map in my case, any `UiKitView` should do),
   - a top "panel" whose box is 80 px high but which lays out 400 px of content inside a `ClipRect`, exactly like a collapsed sheet that keeps its page mounted,
   - a second, smaller platform view inside that content, laid out at y = 120 to 320, so that it falls completely outside the 80 px clip.
2. Run on a physical iPhone or on the simulator. The panel starts closed.
3. Check that nothing of the second platform view is on screen: the clip leaves zero visible pixels of it.
4. Drag the full-screen map inside the horizontal band y = 120 to 320, where the hidden platform view is laid out.
5. Drag the full-screen map below that band, for example at y = 500, and compare.
6. Tap "open panel" and look at the mini map.

The sample below starts with the panel closed and shows the full-screen map's center in the panel header, so the result of each drag is visible without any tooling.

Note that the readout and the button are painted *before* the second platform view on purpose, to keep this report free of the painting issue in #195589.


### Expected results

Touches in that band reach the full-screen map, like everywhere else on screen.

A platform view that its clip has reduced to zero visible pixels should not be hit tested. The clip should constrain the hit area the same way it constrains the pixels.


### Actual results

Drags inside the band do nothing at all: the full-screen map does not pan, and the center readout does not move. Drags below the band pan it normally.

The gestures are not dropped, they are consumed by the invisible platform view. Tap "open panel" after dragging in the band and the mini map has panned by exactly the amount of those drags, so it received every one of them while it had no pixels on screen.

The symptom is the inverse of #195589 (closed as a duplicate of #192245, fixed on beta by the revert): there, the pixels were lost and hit testing was correct. Here the rendering is correct and the hit testing is wrong.

## What I think is happening

Clips on a platform view never reach UIKit hit testing. In `shell/platform/darwin/ios/framework/Source/FlutterPlatformViewsController.mm`, both `kClipRect` and `kClipRRect` are applied the same way, as a `FlutterClippingMaskView` installed as the `maskView` of the `ChildClippingView`:

```objc
case flutter::MutatorType::kClipRect: {
  ...
  [(FlutterClippingMaskView*)clipView.maskView clipRect:(*iter)->GetRect() matrix:transformMatrix];
  break;
}
case flutter::MutatorType::kClipRRect: {
  ...
  [(FlutterClippingMaskView*)clipView.maskView clipRRect:(*iter)->GetRRect() matrix:transformMatrix];
  break;
}
```
That mask is deliberately invisible to touches, in FlutterPlatformViews.mm:

```objc
// In some scenarios, when we add this view as a maskView of the ChildClippingView, iOS added
// this view as a subview of the ChildClippingView.
// This results this view blocking touch events on the ChildClippingView.
// So we should always ignore any touch events sent to this view.
// See https://github.com/flutter/flutter/issues/66044
- (BOOL)pointInside:(CGPoint)point withEvent:(UIEvent*)event {
  return NO;
}
and ChildClippingView answers for its subviews only, without ever consulting the mask:


// The ChildClippingView's frame is the bounding rect of the platform view. we only want touches to
// be hit tested and consumed by this view if they are inside the embedded platform view which could
// be smaller the embedded platform view is rotated.
- (BOOL)pointInside:(CGPoint)point withEvent:(UIEvent*)event {
  for (UIView* view in self.subviews) {
    if ([view pointInside:[self convertPoint:point toView:view] withEvent:event]) {
      return YES;
    }
  }
  return NO;
}
```
So the hit area stays the platform view's full bounding rect whatever the clip does to the pixels, and the fix for #66044 removes the one view that knows the clipped shape from the responder chain.

A possible direction: have ChildClippingView.pointInside: also test the point against the clip paths held by its FlutterClippingMaskView (the information is already there, drawRect: builds the same paths), rather than against the subview frames alone. I have not tried patching the engine, so I may be missing a reason this is harder than it looks.

I only checked this on beta; the code quoted above has been in place for years, so I would not expect this to be a regression.

