### Use case

The use-case is explained in the (non-builder) go_router docs:

>Merged application-defined metadata for the current route match.
>
>Metadata is inherited from parent routes, and child routes override parent values with the same key. For example, if a parent route has `{'section': 'library', 'requiresAuth': true}` and the matched child route has` {'requiresAuth': false, 'title': 'Preview'}`, this value is `{'section': 'library', 'requiresAuth': false, 'title': 'Preview'}`.

This isn't exposed to go routes created with `@TypedGoRoute` annotations, which is a shame as it's a very useful tool!

### Proposal

The simplest method would be the superclass providing a `Map<String, dynamic> metadata` prop to be overridden. The generated goroute wouldn't need much work to support that, either:

```dart
@TypedGoRoute<PrivateRoute>(path: '/private')
class PrivateRoute extends GoRouteData with $PrivateRoute {

  /// Metadata for this route, using Map
  @override
  Map<String, dynamic> get metadata => const {
    'requiresAuthentication': true,
    'analyticsName': 'private',
  };

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, GoRouterState state) =>
      const PrivatePage();
}
```

More advanced, you could add a new annotation `@RouteMetadata`, which could mark a privately-scoped property as metadata in type-safe manner:

```dart
@TypedGoRoute<PrivateRoute>(path: '/private')
@RouteMetadata<String>('analyticsName')
@RouteMetadata<bool>('requiresAuthentication')
class PrivateRoute extends GoRouteData with $PrivateRoute {

  @override
  String get analyticsName => 'private';

  @override
  bool get requiresAuthentication => true;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context, GoRouterState state) =>
      const PrivatePage();
}

```

There's loads of approaches. Some less type-safe than others, but the fact is that "Type safe" go router isn't type safe at all once you have to manage stuff like the top-level `onEnter` handler or `GoRouterState` which use `RouteBase` as their source of truth. I understand the limitations at play, of course, hence why I'm not massively fussed if this is "type safe" in practice.

