### Standalone physical-device attempts (all Impeller/OpenGLES)

The diagnostic app has a separate application ID AND namespace: `com.dailylinesoft.diagnostics.bible_renderer_probe`. It has no Firebase packages, database, storage permissions, user data, or hardware-specific renderer opt-out. Every asset is synthetic. These tests do not access or alter the real application's records or reminders.

| Probe | Workload | Observed result |
|---|---|---|
| A | 120 batches × 33 synthetic PNG decodes, offscreen image painting | Completed 3,960 requested decodes; no native abort |
| B | Onscreen RawImage rendering, 8 concurrent double decodes with intermediate disposal, resizing, 600 offscreen paints/readbacks | Completed 9,600 decodes and 600 readbacks; no native abort |
| C | Three parallel startup branches: 33 JPEGs, 12 RGBA WebPs, one differently sized JPEG; ImageProvider cloning/disposal, rounded clipping, medium filtering, Picture.toImage, cache clearing | 30-round run completed; four further cold starts also completed; no native abort |

One repeated Probe C monitoring window initially timed out after round 25; the same process subsequently logged round 30 and completion. It is **not** counted as a crash. Probe C mirrors a two-second application warm-up timeout, so its counters describe requested work rather than independently instrumented successful GPU uploads.

Earlier separate Skia controls also completed, but that does not establish that every Impeller run fails or prove a root cause.

