`material_ui` and `cupertino_ui` tests run against more than one Flutter version (at least `master` and `stable`). When a framework change in flutter/flutter changes behavior that these tests check, no single test expectation can pass on both channels. We should add a small test helper, such as `flutterVersionIsAtLeast(...)`, so tests can expect different results depending on the Flutter version they run on.

## Prior art

DWDS has a helper for this exact problem, but for Dart SDK versions:

- Definition: [dart-lang/sdk `pkg/dwds_test_common/lib/utilities.dart`](https://github.com/dart-lang/sdk/blob/main/pkg/dwds_test_common/lib/utilities.dart)
- Example usage: [`evaluate_common.dart#L370`](https://github.com/dart-lang/webdev/blob/f702fd36f4711b47e4a34092b47edf6a0b037c7c/dwds/test/integration/evaluate_common.dart#L370)

## Proposal

Add an equivalent helper based on [`FlutterVersion`](https://api.flutter.dev/flutter/services/FlutterVersion-class.html) (`package:flutter/services.dart`). The Flutter tool fills it in with `--dart-define`s (`FLUTTER_VERSION`, `FLUTTER_FRAMEWORK_REVISION`, and so on) for every command that builds `BuildInfo`, including `flutter test`.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/services.dart' show FlutterVersion;
import 'package:pub_semver/pub_semver.dart';

/// Whether the tests are running against a Flutter framework whose version is
/// at least [version].
///
/// Use this to switch test expectations around framework changes that have
/// not yet reached every channel these tests run on, then remove the check
/// once the package's minimum Flutter version is >= [version].
bool flutterVersionIsAtLeast(String version) {
  final String? current = FlutterVersion.version;
  if (current == null) {
    throw StateError(
      'FlutterVersion.version is unavailable; '
      'run these tests with `flutter test`.',
    );
  }
  return Version.parse(current) >= Version.parse(version);
}
```

Example usage:

```dart
// TODO(someone): Remove branching once the minimum Flutter version includes
// https://github.com/flutter/flutter/pull/#.
expect(
   find.byKey(key, skipOffstage: false), flutterVersionIsAtLeast('3.XX.0-YY.0.pre') 
     ? findsNWidgets(newCount)
     : findsNWidgets(oldCount)
);
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Build the usual "sticky section headers" list: a `CustomScrollView` where each section is a `SliverMainAxisGroup` that starts with a pinned `SliverPersistentHeader`, so the end of each section pushes its header off the screen while the next section's header gets pinned.
2. Scroll until the second section's header is pinned and the first section's header has been pushed completely out of view, while the end of the first section is still within the cache extent above the viewport.
3. Look at the semantics tree, for example with `tester.semantics.simulatedAccessibilityTraversal()` (code sample below, run with `flutter test`), or with VoiceOver on iOS.

### Expected results

Once the group has pushed its pinned header completely out of view, the header leaves the semantics tree, like any other sliver that is no longer visible.

### Actual results

The pushed-out header stays in the semantics tree until the whole group leaves the cache extent. It is flagged as hidden and, as a pinned header, tagged with `RenderViewport.excludeFromScrolling`, so it sits outside the scrollable's node and comes first in the traversal, before the header that is actually pinned:

```
[Morning, Afternoon, Morning item 6, Morning item 7, Morning item 8, Afternoon item 1, ...]
```

On iOS, VoiceOver can focus hidden nodes that have a label (`isFocusable` in `SemanticsObject.mm`), so with "Afternoon" pinned it can reach an invisible "Morning" header before anything else on the screen. Focusing it also sends `showOnScreen` (`accessibilityElementDidBecomeFocused` does that for hidden and header nodes), which runs into #177453 and scrolls the list back to the start of the "Morning" section. On Android, hidden nodes are not exposed to TalkBack, so it is not affected.

**Cause.** When the group's remaining extent is smaller than the paint extent of its children, `RenderSliverMainAxisGroup.performLayout` moves the pinned children up with a paint correction. `visitChildrenForSemantics` only checks `child.geometry!.visible || child.geometry!.cacheExtent > 0.0 || child.ensureSemantics`, and the pinned child still reports the positive `paintExtent` of its own layout, so it is visited even after the correction has pushed it out of view. This code is the same on `master`.

**Workaround.** We use a `SliverMainAxisGroup` subclass whose render object skips, in `visitChildrenForSemantics`, the pinned children that end at or before `constraints.overlap` after the correction. It makes the test below pass:

```dart
class _RenderGroup extends RenderSliverMainAxisGroup {
  @override
  void visitChildrenForSemantics(RenderObjectVisitor visitor) {
    super.visitChildrenForSemantics((RenderObject child) {
      if (!_isPushedOut(child as RenderSliver)) {
        visitor(child);
      }
    });
  }

  // A pinned child that the group has pushed out of view: it only paints
  // behind the slivers pinned above the group, or above the viewport.
  bool _isPushedOut(RenderSliver child) {
    final SliverGeometry geometry = child.geometry!;
    if (geometry.maxScrollObstructionExtent == 0 || geometry.paintExtent == 0) {
      return false;
    }
    final Offset offset = (child.parentData! as SliverPhysicalParentData).paintOffset;
    final double? start = switch (constraints.axisDirection) {
      AxisDirection.down => offset.dy,
      AxisDirection.right => offset.dx,
      AxisDirection.up || AxisDirection.left => null,
    };
    return start != null &&
        start + geometry.paintExtent <= constraints.overlap + precisionErrorTolerance;
  }
}
```

Related: #177453 (`showOnScreen` from a pinned header inside a `SliverMainAxisGroup` resets the scroll position). It was reported with a `TextField`, but on iOS VoiceOver triggers it whenever a pinned section header gets focus, since headers always get `showOnScreen` on focus.

