### Steps to reproduce

1. Create an app with `flutter_gpu` enabled on Android, and force the OpenGL ES backend in `AndroidManifest.xml`:
   ```xml
   <meta-data android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.EnableFlutterGPU" android:value="true" />
   <meta-data android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.ImpellerBackend" android:value="opengles" />
   ```
   Devices that fall back to GLES on their own behave the same way.
2. Call `gpu.Texture.fromImage` **once** on a rasterized image, then just animate. Code sample below.
3. Build in release, run it, and sample `adb shell dumpsys meminfo <package>` every 10 s.
4. For the control, run the same app built with `--dart-define=WRAP=false`, which skips the single `fromImage` call.

### Expected results

Memory stays flat after the one call, as in the control run.

### Actual results

`Graphics` and `Native Heap` grow from the moment of that single call, and keep growing while the app renders frames. After about a minute, garbage pixels appear at the top of the screen while the animation keeps running.

Getac ZX10 (Snapdragon 660, Adreno 512, `OpenGL ES 3.2 V@0502.0`), Android 12, release build, 10 s samples:

| Run | Graphics (MB) | Native Heap (MB) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| control, no `fromImage` | 59.8 → 60.3 over 3 min, flat | 14.9 → 14.9 |
| one `fromImage` call | 65.5 → 70.6 → 75.7 → 80.7 → 86.3 → 91.4 in the first ~55 s (≈470 KB/s) | 24.4 → 31.8 → 40.4 → 48.8 → 58.1 in the first ~45 s |

After ~55 s, Graphics plateaus near 91 MB, native heap collapses to ~3 MB, and rendering corruption starts (screenshot attached).

In a real app that renders continuously, we saw the same steady growth (≈250 KB/s Graphics plus ≈270 KB/s native, idle) until the low-memory killer ended the process at ~650 MB PSS. The same app on Vulkan devices (Mali-G68, Adreno 810) stays flat.

### Why (source at master `7b0a985e54`, paths under `engine/src/flutter`)

1. `Texture.fromImage` calls `GetTextureFromImage` twice: `lib/gpu/texture.cc:293` for the texture info, `:343` to wrap.
2. Each call builds a throwaway `impeller::AiksContext` on the UI thread (`lib/gpu/texture.cc:179`).
3. Its `ContentContext` constructor creates a `HostBuffer` tied to the context's shared `GpuSubmissionTracker`, and submits a blit for the 1×1 empty texture (`impeller/entity/contents/content_context.cc:563-603`).
4. On GLES that submit runs on the UI thread, which has no current GL context. `CommandBufferGLES::OnSubmitCommands` records the submission, then queues its completion as a deferred reactor operation (`impeller/renderer/backend/gles/command_buffer_gles.cc:74-119`).
5. `ReactorGLES` queues operations per thread, and only the queuing thread runs them (`reactor_gles.cc:173-187`, `:377-381`). The UI thread never reacts, so the completion is never recorded.
6. `GpuSubmissionTracker::CompletedThrough()` is a watermark (`impeller/core/gpu_submission_tracker.h`), so it stays at that submission for good.
7. From then on, every `HostBuffer::Reset` treats the next arena entry as still in flight: it moves the entry's buffers to `retired_buffers_`, which are never released, and allocates a fresh 1 MB block (`impeller/core/host_buffer.cc:234-279`). Every frame leaks its transient buffers.

`flutter_gpu`'s own `CommandBuffer::submit` posts to the raster thread on GLES (`lib/gpu/command_buffer.cc:187-212`), which is why only `fromImage` triggers this.

**Related, on Vulkan:** the same throwaway context's `HostBuffer` destructor calls `IdleWaiterVK::WaitIdle()`, i.e. `vkDeviceWaitIdle`, on the UI thread, twice per `fromImage` call (`impeller/core/host_buffer.cc:50-55`, `impeller/renderer/backend/vulkan/context_vk.h:49-62`).
- On a Mali-G68 tablet that blocked the UI thread for 2–6 ms per call when idle, and up to 35 ms while frames were in flight (avg 17 ms).
- `vkDeviceWaitIdle` requires external synchronization of all queues, and the raster thread's concurrent submits don't provide it.

Getting the `impeller::Texture` out of `DlImageImpeller` without constructing an `AiksContext` would avoid both problems.

